Quoting  Carl  <carlhnelson@gmail.com>:
>On Oct 1, 6:46=A0am, David Damerell <damer...@chiark.greenend.org.uk>
>>You probably want to quaff (with minimal inventory) from any fountain not
>>confirmed non-magical.
>But for the purposes of acquiring a wish, the odds of getting a wish
>and the odds of the fountain drying up are the same.  Or does whether
>the fountain is magical affect anything?  If they're both the same,
>then I prefer to dip since quaffing has annoying negative potential.

A magic fountain never dries up; it will always eventually end in the stat
gain. The real question is what additional negative effects you risk when
quaffing rather than dipping in order to establish that a fountain is not
magical (if you dip in a magic fountain, you waste the stat gain).

The extra adverse effect from quaffing is the 1/30 chance of cursing 1/5
of your inventory. The other adverse effects are also seen with dipping,
so not a difference, and most of them (like water moccasins) are no
problem for a midgame character.

If you quaff from a non-magic fountain, 9/30 of the time you get a "cool
draught". 1/3 of the time it dries up and you know it wasn't magic, but
otherwise you quaff again. Hence there is a 3/30 chance you stop quaffing
because of this case.

The remaining 21/30 of the time, you establish it is not a magic fountain.
In 1/21 of those cases, you get the curse effect. If you are carrying a
minimal inventory of 5 items (armour, say), that curses an average of one
item. 

Hence there is overall a 1/24 chance that you get the curse effect because
you chose to quaff not dip until you found out if the fountain was magical
or not.

1/7 of fountains are magical. Therefore using this strategy on seven
fountains gains you one stat gain on average (to all stats with Luck 4
or higher, and in the middle game you should have such luck). It also
gets you 6 * 1/24 of a cursed item, which is 1/4. 

So if you quaff from fountains you do not know are non-magic, you do not
reduce the chance of getting a wish, and you on average will consume one
potion of holy water uncursing objects for every 4 stat gains you get; if
your armour was blessed and you desire it reblessed, you are still using
one potion for every 2 stat gains you get.

That's a good trade in my book - any time you quaff a blessed potion
of gain ability rather than diluting it, you effectively consume a positon
of holy water for _one_ stat gain (remembering that we're always talking
about a bonus to all six stats in either case).
